Removal of guest molecules within the MOF materials’ pores began with a solvent exchange procedure that included immersing the as-synthesized samples into anhydrous DMF (typically $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and refreshing the DMF three times over the course of three days. Further solvent exchange, but this time with low boiling point solvents, was carried out in a similar fashion (three times over the course of three days). For typical activation,11 a MOF sample was fully exchanged with chloroform or dichloromethane $\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{(10~mL)}}$ and evacuated at room temperature for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ and at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for an additional $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For supercritical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ drying (SCD) activation,12 a MOF sample was fully immersed in anhydrous acetone and thoroughly exchanged with liquid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the chamber of a Tousimis Samdri PVT-3D critical point dryer. The sample was subsequently kept in a supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere (typical conditions of $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $1200\mathrm{{psi}},$ ) for $30\mathrm{min}$ and then the supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was slowly vented at room temperature over the course of several hours.
